00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:05.880
Fast fact number one, this episode was written by Gene Stepnitzky and Lee Eisenberg.

191
00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.040
I spoke with both of them, Angela.

192
00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:15.000
Gene told me that the inspiration for this episode was the Edward Albee play, Who's Afraid

193
00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:15.980
of Virginia Woolf?

194
00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:22.200
Now, if you guys don't know this play, it is amazing, and it is about a crazy older married

195
00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:27.620
couple who invites a younger couple from their work over for a dinner party.

196
00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:31.360
And it's just this insane evening.

197
00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:33.240
It completely goes off the rails.

198
00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:37.960
There's a movie version starring Elizabeth Taylor and Richard Burton that is crazy good.

199
00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:38.820
You can check it out.

200
00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:40.600
If you liked this episode, you'll like that movie.

201
00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.000
Here's a little fun fact from Lee Eisenberg.

202
00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:48.340
The original working title for this episode was Who's Afraid of Jan Levinson Gould?

203
00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:55.340
Which makes total sense to me because Jan Levinson Gould really brings it.

204
00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:58.420
She scared the bejesus out of me in this episode.

205
00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:02.080
There are a few moments where she looks at Pam where I was like, I would not want to be

206
00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:02.740
Pam today.

207
00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:05.160
For sure.

208
00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:12.080
Jean told me that they were assigned this script right before the writers went on their summer

209
00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:14.480
hiatus in June of 2007.

210
00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:19.580
So guys, we would go on this little hiatus every summer, and the writers could go off

211
00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:20.580
and write episodes.

212
00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:24.240
Normally, writers only got one week to write an episode.

213
00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:31.560
But because they were on hiatus, they had three weeks to work on this episode.

214
00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.660
I mean, they only got paid for one.

215
00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:35.460
But they said they couldn't put it down.

216
00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.480
They just kept adding details and details.

217
00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:39.280
They used their whole hiatus to write it.

218
00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:41.320
I mean, I think it shows.

219
00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:44.580
I don't want to change the whole structure of how we do television.

220
00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.320
Maybe we should all get three weeks for a script.

221
00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:47.920
Just saying.

222
00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:50.560
So we had a fan question from Megan W.

223
00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:51.660
