243
00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:29.280
So hearing from this man who was our mentor telling us we nailed it, it was the highest

244
00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:31.280
compliment he ever received as a writer.

245
00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:33.880
I bet that means so much to them.

246
00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:37.340
You know, Jenna, here's what I remember about this table read.

247
00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:40.900
I do remember this build.

248
00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:44.980
It started and it just took over the room.

249
00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:48.380
People were laughing and then laughing and then really laughing.

250
00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:54.420
And by the end, that final line of the script, everyone started applauding.

251
00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:57.500
And I remember the applause lasted for like a minute.

252
00:12:57.880 --> 00:12:58.260
Yeah.

253
00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:03.500
It must have felt so amazing for Gene and Lee to see their hard work,

254
00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:06.440
just have that kind of adoration in the moment.

255
00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:08.160
Well, get this, guys.

256
00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:13.620
I guess the folks at NBC did not love this script.

257
00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:14.820
Oh, no.

258
00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:19.580
After the table read, Greg had a phone call with NBC.

259
00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:20.740
Uh-oh.

260
00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:25.280
When we asked Rainn Wilson about this episode, he talks about that phone call.

261
00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:32.920
Well, Dinner Party, it is one of the top three all-time great office episodes.

262
00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:35.480
It's absolutely extraordinary.

263
00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:37.560
It's a pretty dark episode.

264
00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:43.240
And after the table read, I heard a story from Greg Daniels that the network was like,

265
00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:45.980
um, that's a very dark episode.

266
00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:48.900
And Greg was like, yes, yes, it is.

267
00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:52.300
They're like, are we sure we want it so dark?

268
00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:54.240
And Greg was like, yep.

269
00:13:54.740 --> 00:13:57.100
And they were like, okay.

270
00:13:58.160 --> 00:13:59.360
And then we proceeded.

271
00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:04.400
You know, I feel like if this episode had come earlier in our run,

272
00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:06.460
maybe it wouldn't have happened, right?

273
00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:11.340
Maybe NBC would have given notes, and Greg would have maybe had to sort of pick his battles.

274
00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:16.500
But at this point, our show was so established and critically acclaimed and had won awards,

275
00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:22.460
I feel like Greg was able to confidently say to them, like, yeah, this is the show.

276
00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:24.340
This is how it's written, and we're going to do it.

277
00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:28.880
Jean and Lee told me that they did not do a rewrite on the script.

278
00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:29.620
Wow.

279
00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:35.080
You guys, all of our episodes, as amazing as they were, would go through several rewrites.

280
00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:37.800
That's just kind of how a script comes to be.

281
00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:42.680
So for this to not have a single rewrite, that is amazing.

282
00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:43.560
Yeah.

283
00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:45.920
So we had this amazing script.

284
00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:49.340
We're all ready to shoot this amazing episode.

285
00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:53.720
And then we got shut down because of the writer's strike.

286
00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:54.660
Yeah.

287
00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:57.320
For four months, we had to wait.

288
00:14:57.320 --> 00:15:05.620
It was just a very surreal time because so many shows that shut down did not come back.

289
00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:08.280
They weren't able to survive the strike.

290
00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:13.460
And the fact that we were able to come back and we knew we had scripts waiting for us was huge.

291
00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:18.700
But it was really important for us as a cast that we stand with our writers and support them.

292
00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:28.500
Gene and Lee said it was really surreal because they went from writing this episode, having this table read, to picketing their own set.

293
00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:29.020
Yeah.

294
00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:31.040
And it was so, so bizarre.

295
00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:32.240
But we shut down.

296
00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:35.020
This leads me to fast fact number two.

297
00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:35.840
Mm-hmm.

298
00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:44.440
Fast fact number two is that Paul Feig directed this episode, but he almost did not direct this episode.

299
00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:46.820
I couldn't even imagine if it wasn't him.

300
00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:48.000
I know.

301
00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:48.940
I know.

302
00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:53.880
Well, when we reached out to him, he told us how this came about.

303
00:15:54.700 --> 00:15:56.780
I had been brought on.

304
00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:59.260
I directed a lot of episodes of The Office.

305
00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:06.840
But in that season, I had been given Survivorman, which I was really excited about and actually very, very proud of that episode.

306
00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:12.320
But I remember reading the script for The Dinner Party and it was given to some other director.

307
00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:16.380
And I was so jealous because I thought, like, if only I could do this episode.

308
00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.220
This is, like, everything I want to do.

309
00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:21.380
It's so uncomfortable and cringy and hilarious.

310
00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:23.780
But another director was assigned to it.

311
00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:25.720
But then the writer's strike happened.

312
00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:32.100
And so it all fell apart because I did Survivorman and then the writer's strike stopped everything.

313
00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:35.860
And so we were off for months and months and months, however long the writer's strike went.

314
00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:44.520
And my great joy was at the end of that, everything shifted so much that that director who was supposed to do the dinner party wasn't able to.

315
00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:46.280
And so it came back around to me.

316
00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:50.180
And so I was so excited to do that episode.

317
00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:55.260
I just saw it so clearly in my head and it was just so funny to me.

318
00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:59.780
But the crazy thing about that episode was once we did it, we knew it was so funny.

319
00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:04.360
The audience, the loyal office audiences hated it.

320
00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:13.580
It was really a very divisive episode because I think it was so uncomfortable and so cringy that people couldn't deal with it.

321
00:17:13.580 --> 00:17:17.140
And I remember like we were so excited, all of us, all the writers, everybody,

322
00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:21.080
we were so excited because we really thought this was kind of like one of the best things the show could do.

323
00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:25.100
And we were kind of all bummed out that people were so weird about it.

324
00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:30.820
But I think the whole thing was it was so uncomfortable that the first time you watch it, you almost can't handle it.

325
00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:36.860
But once you watch it a second and third and fourth and however many times you watch it, then it becomes fun because you know it's coming.

326
00:17:37.180 --> 00:17:40.380
You know it's going to be terrible and you just can enjoy the cringiness.

327
00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:43.900
And so I could not be prouder of that episode.

328
00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:49.440
It's really, you know, I won a DGA directing award for it, which is the only time I'll ever get an award for anything.

329
00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:51.900
But I just think it's the funniest thing in the world.

330
00:17:52.440 --> 00:18:01.220
I remember the fan response to this episode was not overwhelmingly positive when the show first aired.

331
00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:07.600
Yeah, I remember even within my own extended family, they either loved it or they hated it.

332
00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:09.960
Yes, very strong reactions.

333
00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:12.120
It has grown to be beloved.

334
00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:14.220
It was not beloved when it first aired.

335
00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:19.680
You know, I don't know who this other director was that was supposed to direct this episode.

336
00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:25.060
But Gene told me that this person had never directed a single camera comedy ever.

337
00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:27.960
That this episode would have been their first one.

338
00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:29.240
Oh, my Lord.

339
00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:36.060
Now, maybe it was a big time movie person, you know, and they'd just never done half hour TV before.

340
00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:39.160
I am so glad that we got Paul Feig.

341
00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:45.480
Like, yeah, I mean, you don't want the dinner party script to be your first time at single camera comedy.

342
00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:47.640
If I were that person, I wouldn't sleep.

343
00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:48.280
Yeah.

344
00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:49.400
No.

345
00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:55.280
Fast fact number three, Angela, we know that the writer's strike provided us with Paul Feig.

346
00:18:55.280 --> 00:19:00.000
It also provided us with a problem in the form of your giant belly.

347
00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:03.180
I was wondering when you're going to bring that up.

348
00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:10.980
I'm not the only person bringing up your belly, Angela, because when we asked Paul Feig about his memories of shooting this episode, he discussed it.

349
00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:12.240
Oh, no.

350
00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:22.180
One of my favorites is how we had to desperately hide Angela's pregnancy during it because she was very late, late stages.

351
00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:30.740
And so you'll notice a lot of her carrying flowers and things in the foreground and then a pillow on her lap when she's sitting on the couch.

352
00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:33.160
I think we had her in a big, giant overcoat, too.

353
00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:34.740
So that was fun.

354
00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:37.740
It was very challenging, doing special effects with no special effects.

355
00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:46.500
So, Angela, we also got mail about your belly from Susie S., Alex G., and Emma O.

356
00:19:46.700 --> 00:19:47.840
Oh, no, ladies.

357
00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:57.360
They want to know how far along were you in your pregnancy and what kind of meeting was there for hiding Angela's baby bump in this episode.

358
00:19:57.520 --> 00:19:58.560
Please tell us everything.

359
00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:03.820
Well, ladies, if memory serves me, we filmed this episode in early March.

360
00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:08.080
Now, you have to remember, when we did the deposition, I was barely showing.

361
00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:10.840
Four months have gone by into my pregnancy.

362
00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:13.040
I had my baby early May.

363
00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:15.880
So it was a belly.

364
00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:18.360
It was a full-on baby belly.

365
00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:21.820
And I do remember poor Paul Feig.

366
00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:25.040
He's the sweetest person coming up to me and being like,

367
00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:29.600
Hey, Angela, so we're going to have you stand behind the copier.

368
00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:31.220
We're going to have you hold this pillow.

369
00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:32.180
We're going to have you hold a purse.

370
00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:33.000
We're going to have flowers.

371
00:20:33.160 --> 00:20:33.900
We're going to have a wall.

372
00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:34.580
You stand behind.

373
00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:36.200
There were like all of these things.

374
00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:39.760
There were actual meetings about how to hide my belly.

375
00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:47.220
And then when we started filming, I remember tucking behind the copier and Matt Soane, our

376
00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:50.000
camera operator, saying, I can still see it, Ange.

377
00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:50.860
I was like, what?

378
00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:55.840
So now there was this new dialogue I had with the camera operators where they'd be like,

379
00:20:55.960 --> 00:21:00.460
back it up a little, pivot, pivot, duck, hold it higher.

380
00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:03.340
We had this whole language of the belly hide.

381
00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:08.780
Am I correct that in that scene when you first enter the condo and Michael and Jan are trying

382
00:21:08.780 --> 00:21:13.060
to take your coats, you don't take off your coat because you needed it to hide your belly?

383
00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:14.000
Oh, yeah.

384
00:21:14.180 --> 00:21:15.920
And my coat was enormous.

385
00:21:16.220 --> 00:21:17.720
It's like not a coat I would ever wear.

386
00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:19.460
It was so big.

387
00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:25.180
But yeah, I have a lot of track the belly moments for this episode that I can sprinkle in as

388
00:21:25.180 --> 00:21:25.580
we go.

389
00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:26.900
But we had to get creative.

390
00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:28.100
It was a big belly.

391
00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:29.400
One day, no belly.

392
00:21:29.540 --> 00:21:30.420
Next day, big belly.

393
00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:32.460
That's what happened during the writer's strike.

394
00:21:32.680 --> 00:21:35.020
Well, guys, those are my fast facts.

395
00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:40.340
I think we should take a break and we will be back with a dinner party and more audio

396
00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:42.240
clips from our dinner guests.

397
00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:43.520
Light your candles.

398
00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:45.160
Get your osso buco going.

399
00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:46.740
We're about to have a dinner party.

400
00:21:57.840 --> 00:21:59.000
We are back.

401
00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:00.580
We are at the office.

402
00:22:00.820 --> 00:22:02.060
It's a Friday night.

403
00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:03.640
We're working late.

404
00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:07.240
Oh, there's a special assignment from corporate,

405
00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:10.360
which we never find out what the special assignment is.

406
00:22:10.460 --> 00:22:14.920
Like, really and truly, how creative did Michael Scott have to get that he was able

407
00:22:14.920 --> 00:22:17.000
to con the whole bullpen?

408
00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:19.480
Yeah, everyone's shuffling papers.

409
00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:21.120
They're looking at things.

410
00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:22.300
What are we doing?

411
00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:24.340
What do we think the assignment is?

412
00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:26.380
It seems like we're doing stuff.

413
00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:31.560
I feel like this is Michael's smartest moment, that he came up with a special assignment that

414
00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:33.200
everyone believed.

415
00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:34.360
Yeah.

416
00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:38.560
At 36 seconds, Jenna, is when Jim knows.

417
00:22:38.740 --> 00:22:43.640
I believe this is when Jim knows because Michael gets on the phone to corporate and he's like,

418
00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:45.940
it's not fair to these people.

419
00:22:46.160 --> 00:22:49.340
These people are my friends and I care about them.

420
00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:51.940
Like, who talks like that to corporate?

421
00:22:52.340 --> 00:22:52.900
I know.

422
00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:53.760
Jim knows.

423
00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:59.140
But people are grumbling, they're griping, and there is a great deleted scene.

424
00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:01.140
It's a whole runner with Toby.

425
00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:06.880
Toby is sharing that he's so bummed out because he had tickets to Tom Petty.

426
00:23:07.400 --> 00:23:07.860
What?

427
00:23:08.180 --> 00:23:08.560
Yeah.

428
00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:13.720
And Toby had to give them to his ex-wife and her new boyfriend.

429
00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:15.360
Oh, no.

430
00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:20.360
So, yeah, everyone is kind of like, they're bummed out.

431
00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:21.780
Why are they staying so late?

432
00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:25.700
And then Michael proudly walks in because he's had this phone call and he's like, you know

433
00:23:25.700 --> 00:23:25.940
what?

434
00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:27.300
You don't have to stay late.

435
00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:29.260
I told corporate we're not doing it.

436
00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:30.660
Everyone can go home.

437
00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:33.100
And they're like, wow, thank you, Michael.

438
00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:34.020
Yeah.

439
00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:41.000
And then he turns to Jim and he says, hey, you want to come have dinner at our place tonight?

440
00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:43.540
I know you have no plans.

441
00:23:44.060 --> 00:23:46.280
Oh, and Jim's like, he got me.

442
00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:46.940
Yep.

443
00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:47.340
Yeah.

444
00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:51.340
Well, there's a series of deleted talking heads where people talk about their ideal

445
00:23:51.340 --> 00:23:52.160
dinner party.

446
00:23:52.300 --> 00:23:56.640
And there's one by Michael Scott that really cracked me up.

447
00:23:56.880 --> 00:23:57.900
Sam, can we play that?

448
00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:00.240
My ideal dinner party.

449
00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:00.680
Easy.

450
00:24:01.040 --> 00:24:07.120
Jim Pam, Ryan, the mayor, Barack Obama, Shiloh Jolie-Pitt.

451
00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:12.080
Because at the end of the night, Brad or Angelina would have to come and pick him up.

452
00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:13.880
And I would get to meet them.

453
00:24:15.180 --> 00:24:17.160
Shia LaBeouf, because of Disturbia.

454
00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:21.900
All of the children of the world.

455
00:24:22.840 --> 00:24:23.840
Val Kilmer.

456
00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:26.020
But he probably wouldn't come.

457
00:24:26.260 --> 00:24:26.780
Too famous.

458
00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:28.840
Obviously, George Clooney.

459
00:24:32.900 --> 00:24:34.720
And Jan, definitely.

460
00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:35.920
If there was room.

461
00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:43.380
Michael would invite all of the children of the world, Val Kilmer, definitely George Clooney,

462
00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:45.420
but only Jan if there was room.

463
00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:48.160
Oh, that tells you everything you need to know.

464
00:24:48.400 --> 00:24:48.620
Right?

465
00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:53.080
And I feel like they deleted that because it was tipping his hand too soon.

466
00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:53.640
Right?

467
00:24:56.090 --> 00:25:01.510
Well, now we have a very sad talking head from Dwight that ends with just crying.

468
00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:04.030
He's so sad he hasn't been invited.

469
00:25:04.390 --> 00:25:04.630
Yeah.

470
00:25:04.930 --> 00:25:08.110
I thought Rain was so funny in this talking head.

471
00:25:08.270 --> 00:25:09.390
He really made me laugh.

472
00:25:09.550 --> 00:25:10.170
Bless his heart.

473
00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:11.330
All right, lady.

474
00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:13.010
It's time to go to the condo.

475
00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:13.630
All right.

476
00:25:13.810 --> 00:25:16.130
So Pam and Jim arrive to the condo.

477
00:25:16.350 --> 00:25:17.670
It's already awkward.

478
00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:21.770
And this is going to be a comedy sprint to the end.

479
00:25:21.770 --> 00:25:24.990
It's just going to be one joke after the next.

480
00:25:25.270 --> 00:25:31.370
Well, it starts with both Michael and Jan trying to hug Jim at the same time.

481
00:25:31.570 --> 00:25:34.830
And then I don't know if you noticed, but only Michael hugs Pam.

482
00:25:35.170 --> 00:25:37.190
Jan does not even go in for a hug.

483
00:25:37.330 --> 00:25:38.070
Oh, I noticed.

484
00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:42.050
And then she throws you some serious shade about the wine you brought.

485
00:25:42.310 --> 00:25:44.130
She's like, oh, thank you so much.

486
00:25:44.250 --> 00:25:45.510
This will be great to cook with.

487
00:25:45.650 --> 00:25:48.870
Which you guys know is code for thanks for the crappy wine.

488
00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:50.190
Exactly.

489
00:25:51.650 --> 00:25:58.070
Angela, Gene told me that one of their goals in this episode was that it would just be loaded

490
00:25:58.070 --> 00:26:03.150
with a ton of subtle passive-aggressive comments and coded language.

491
00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:07.270
And that this dig at Pam about the wine was the firSt.

492
00:26:07.690 --> 00:26:14.550
Gene also told me that this moment was inspired by the fact that he and Lee were roommates when

493
00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:16.430
they first started writing on the show.

494
00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:19.770
And that Lee Eisenberg is an amazing cook.

495
00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:21.630
He referred to him as a gourmand.

496
00:26:22.090 --> 00:26:22.510
What?

497
00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:23.470
Yeah.

498
00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:28.670
And one time, Gene saw Lee cooking with some wine, which was something that Gene did not

499
00:26:28.670 --> 00:26:29.450
know you could do.

500
00:26:29.570 --> 00:26:32.430
His actual words were, it blew my mind.

501
00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:33.890
He's such a dude.

502
00:26:34.190 --> 00:26:36.150
Of course you can cook with wine.

503
00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:40.530
Well, I guess Lee explained to him that it has to be a cheap wine.

504
00:26:40.530 --> 00:26:43.930
And that always stuck in his head that you cook with cheap wine.

505
00:26:43.930 --> 00:26:46.230
And that's why he gave that line to Jan.

506
00:26:46.570 --> 00:26:47.610
Well, it's perfect.

507
00:26:48.370 --> 00:26:50.130
Babe, should we take a tour, babe?

508
00:26:50.450 --> 00:26:51.030
Yeah, babe.

509
00:26:51.330 --> 00:26:53.770
Oh, I'm so sorry about this horrendous carpet.

510
00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:54.990
We're a work in progress.

511
00:26:57.290 --> 00:26:59.290
I call this the mask wall, babe.

512
00:27:00.230 --> 00:27:03.630
Oh, we watch this episode a lot, but it's so good.

513
00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:09.870
This home tour really kicks off all of the Jan and Michael dynamics in this episode.

514
00:27:10.070 --> 00:27:16.230
And when we asked John Krasinski about his memories of this episode, he talked about this

515
00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:19.630
scene of Jim and Pam arriving and then starting the tour.

516
00:27:19.630 --> 00:27:25.630
That episode was without a doubt one of my favorite to ever shoot because there was this incredible

517
00:27:25.630 --> 00:27:30.450
energy on set, this idea that we were finally getting to pull the curtain back on the world

518
00:27:30.450 --> 00:27:31.330
of Michael Scott.

519
00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:37.830
And I think that for me, I was already almost laughing in every scene because of how Steve

520
00:27:37.830 --> 00:27:39.510
was playing his character.

521
00:27:39.810 --> 00:27:48.650
There was this unbelievable beaming of pride and joy to have these two people finally in

522
00:27:48.650 --> 00:27:56.050
his home after what I imagine to be a lifetime of hoping and wishing that it could be possible

523
00:27:56.050 --> 00:27:56.510
one day.

524
00:27:56.630 --> 00:27:57.490
And here they were.

525
00:27:57.670 --> 00:28:01.550
And not only were they in his home, he was able to give them a tour.

526
00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:09.390
And then the battling dichotomy of Michael's excitement and Jan's sort of weighty judgment

527
00:28:09.390 --> 00:28:14.270
of his house and his excitement was all loaded into every scene.

528
00:28:15.710 --> 00:28:16.810
It's so true.

529
00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:17.610
So true.

530
00:28:17.610 --> 00:28:18.830
So well said, JK.

531
00:28:19.250 --> 00:28:24.690
We got a fan question, Angela, from Cameron W., Linda M., and Monica J.

532
00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:28.410
Did you guys actually film in someone's house?

533
00:28:28.970 --> 00:28:29.670
Oh, yeah.

534
00:28:29.890 --> 00:28:31.950
There was a man that owned this condo.

535
00:28:32.330 --> 00:28:32.770
Yeah.

536
00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:35.810
It was a condo in Woodland Hills, California.

537
00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:42.310
And I remember they told me they had to take out all of that man's furniture and repaint

538
00:28:42.310 --> 00:28:43.030
his walls.

539
00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:47.050
And then they had to put it all back the way he had it.

540
00:28:47.150 --> 00:28:48.450
And that blew my mind.

541
00:28:48.690 --> 00:28:49.490
I know.

542
00:28:49.490 --> 00:28:53.770
In the deposition DVD commentary, Ed talked about this for a minute.

543
00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:59.170
He said when they filmed in the garage, this man's belongings, there were some of them that

544
00:28:59.170 --> 00:29:01.470
they just shoved all into one corner.

545
00:29:01.470 --> 00:29:04.830
And they were like, what is this guy about?

546
00:29:04.990 --> 00:29:06.310
Because guess what he had?

547
00:29:06.550 --> 00:29:06.950
What?

548
00:29:07.150 --> 00:29:11.150
He had all of these professional ping pong player posters.

549
00:29:11.690 --> 00:29:12.210
What?

550
00:29:12.730 --> 00:29:13.170
Yes.

551
00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:16.010
And they had just had all this ping pong in the deposition.

552
00:29:16.010 --> 00:29:17.370
So they were like, oh, my gosh.

553
00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:20.350
And they recognized some of the names because of Dwight's talking head.

554
00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:26.910
And he said, in addition to all the professional ping pong posters, he had a professional ping

555
00:29:26.910 --> 00:29:30.430
pong table that was folded up and sort of wedged in the corner.

556
00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:31.530
Wow.

557
00:29:31.790 --> 00:29:34.730
So this guy obviously was a ping pong enthusiaSt.

558
00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:35.790
We know that.

559
00:29:36.010 --> 00:29:39.570
Maybe he was an amateur uprising professional ping pong player.

560
00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:41.070
Maybe he was a champion.

561
00:29:41.070 --> 00:29:41.770
We don't know.

562
00:29:41.770 --> 00:29:42.590
We don't know.

563
00:29:42.770 --> 00:29:47.290
But yes, he had to move all of his stuff out and cram a bunch of stuff into the corner

564
00:29:47.290 --> 00:29:47.830
of the garage.

565
00:29:47.830 --> 00:29:50.190
And then we had to put it all back for him.

566
00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:56.230
Well, we asked Paul Feig about designing the condo for Michael and Jan.

567
00:29:56.430 --> 00:29:58.310
And here is what he had to say.

568
00:29:59.630 --> 00:30:05.870
For Michael and Jan's condo, I had the benefit of being the person, the director who got to

569
00:30:05.870 --> 00:30:11.370
do Office Olympics, which was where we established Michael and Jan's condo when he bought it.

570
00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:18.130
And so, you know, found this very kind of stark, normal, cookie cutter kind of condo that he

571
00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:20.390
would have with very stark white walls and all that.

572
00:30:20.490 --> 00:30:21.630
And that was really fun.

573
00:30:21.630 --> 00:30:27.470
So when I was lucky enough to get to do this episode and go back to there, the whole concept

574
00:30:27.470 --> 00:30:32.870
of what Jan would have done with that place was really fun to play with.

575
00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:36.730
And I remember Lee and Jean and I got really hung up on the idea that she had a Warhol.

576
00:30:36.730 --> 00:30:41.630
I think they even put it into the script, but that she had done a like a Warhol-esque print

577
00:30:41.630 --> 00:30:44.850
of herself, which you only see briefly when they're in the hallway.

578
00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:50.250
I think when Jim and Pam first arrive and you see it in the stairwell when they go up to

579
00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:57.890
the infamous candle room and it's like this quad Warhol style picture of Jan.

580
00:30:58.270 --> 00:31:03.670
But I just love the idea that we had to show off how Jan had fixed up the place, but also

581
00:31:03.670 --> 00:31:08.030
the idea that Jan had done it, even though she tried to warm it up, it was kind of very

582
00:31:08.030 --> 00:31:08.510
cold.

583
00:31:08.510 --> 00:31:14.190
So we were hung up on the idea of like really like dark gray walls and what art she would

584
00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:14.670
have put up.

585
00:31:14.790 --> 00:31:19.470
And I was very, very hung up on the idea of them putting up a, that Jan put up a kimono

586
00:31:19.470 --> 00:31:24.030
on the wall, which I had seen somewhere and thought, oh, that's kind of a cool decoration,

587
00:31:24.030 --> 00:31:28.110
but it's also not terribly warm at the same time.

588
00:31:28.230 --> 00:31:29.450
And so that made me laugh.

589
00:31:30.430 --> 00:31:37.050
I have to add another little tidbit, Angela, is that I guess they put up a bunch of photos.

590
00:31:37.430 --> 00:31:42.870
You can't really tell when you're watching, but they are all photos of Jan and other men.

591
00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:45.610
There are no photos of Jan and Michael.

592
00:31:45.870 --> 00:31:49.810
And then there's a lot of photos I remember of just Jan by herself.

593
00:31:50.210 --> 00:31:50.770
Yes.

594
00:31:50.950 --> 00:31:54.330
Like in a frame, like she has made it the Jan shrine.

595
00:31:56.410 --> 00:32:02.070
But I love that she has given it her touch, but it's in no way homey.

596
00:32:02.330 --> 00:32:04.690
No, it's just really kind of cold.

597
00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:09.490
And Michael has no space that is his own, except maybe where all of his rejected furniture

598
00:32:09.490 --> 00:32:11.190
items have gone into the garage.

599
00:32:11.670 --> 00:32:11.970
Yeah.

600
00:32:12.170 --> 00:32:14.730
They take Pam and Jim on this tour.

601
00:32:14.930 --> 00:32:20.550
And we learned that Jan has both an office space and a workspace because she can't create

602
00:32:20.550 --> 00:32:22.430
in her office space.

603
00:32:22.430 --> 00:32:26.290
And her desk and her office space look like no one had ever sat there.

604
00:32:26.450 --> 00:32:27.530
It's so sparse.

605
00:32:27.810 --> 00:32:33.310
Oh, at three minutes, 32 seconds, you can see that her desk chair still has the plastic

606
00:32:33.310 --> 00:32:34.450
cover on the arMs.

607
00:32:34.630 --> 00:32:35.570
Oh my gosh.

608
00:32:35.710 --> 00:32:36.130
Fantastic.

609
00:32:36.670 --> 00:32:38.890
That was a little detail that Paul Feig had.

610
00:32:38.970 --> 00:32:40.010
He thought that would be funny.

611
00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:41.630
So he made them leave it on.

612
00:32:41.890 --> 00:32:42.690
That's great.

613
00:32:43.050 --> 00:32:46.890
They put so much thought into every little nook and cranny of that condo.

614
00:32:47.210 --> 00:32:48.930
Should we talk about the candle room?

615
00:32:49.690 --> 00:32:50.210
Yes.

616
00:32:50.210 --> 00:32:53.890
I can't imagine what that smelled like.

617
00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:55.810
It must have been so strong.

618
00:32:56.170 --> 00:32:56.930
Oh, lady.

619
00:32:57.190 --> 00:32:58.810
We got a lot of mail about it.

620
00:32:59.170 --> 00:33:06.330
From Ross C., Sierra S., Allie E., and Emily D., did the candle room actually smell like

621
00:33:06.330 --> 00:33:06.910
candles?

622
00:33:07.490 --> 00:33:08.110
Oh, yeah.

623
00:33:08.370 --> 00:33:12.130
It was a room full of multiple scented candles.

624
00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:14.870
Well, I didn't have to go in the candle room.

625
00:33:15.010 --> 00:33:18.730
And thank goodness, because I was pregnant and so sensitive to smell, I probably would

626
00:33:18.730 --> 00:33:19.410
have been like, ah.

627
00:33:19.770 --> 00:33:22.390
But Paul Feig spoke about the candle room.

628
00:33:22.530 --> 00:33:23.630
And here's the audio clip.

629
00:33:24.370 --> 00:33:25.610
The candle room.

630
00:33:26.590 --> 00:33:30.530
We just knew that we wanted the candle room to be insane.

631
00:33:30.930 --> 00:33:34.270
And the big thing was like, when you're in there, it smelled crazy.

632
00:33:34.610 --> 00:33:36.150
And it was like, how do we do that?

633
00:33:36.390 --> 00:33:38.130
How do we put that across to an audience?

634
00:33:38.290 --> 00:33:42.030
Because one thing an audience can't take in, the two things, they can't take in the temperature

635
00:33:42.030 --> 00:33:44.650
of what's happening and they can't take in the smell of what's happening.

636
00:33:44.890 --> 00:33:48.090
And so that's why you have that shot of John Krasinski, like kind of like looking out

637
00:33:48.090 --> 00:33:52.610
the door and you're like, oh, like you can't breathe because you just know it just stinks

638
00:33:52.610 --> 00:33:53.090
in there.

639
00:33:53.090 --> 00:33:57.450
But I really just loved how dense and terrible we made that room.

640
00:33:58.530 --> 00:34:02.730
I loved John's look to camera when he was gasping for air.

641
00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:05.470
It was so believable and funny.

642
00:34:05.670 --> 00:34:10.370
And I don't think that was probably an easy thing to convey, but he did it so well.

643
00:34:10.850 --> 00:34:11.690
So well.

644
00:34:11.970 --> 00:34:14.750
John and I talked with Paul before we shot that scene.

645
00:34:14.890 --> 00:34:19.870
And he said, I want it to look like you guys are doing that thing when you walk into

646
00:34:19.870 --> 00:34:24.850
a stinky public restroom and you've taken like a deep breath before you walk in.

647
00:34:25.010 --> 00:34:27.330
You're trying to hold it for as long as you can.

648
00:34:27.450 --> 00:34:29.350
And then you need air.

649
00:34:29.570 --> 00:34:34.310
So you're just trying to take in the smallest amount of air that will get you through.

650
00:34:34.990 --> 00:34:35.210
Yeah.

651
00:34:35.710 --> 00:34:38.850
So that's what we were trying to do in that scene.

652
00:34:39.330 --> 00:34:41.650
Well, now they move into the master bedroom.

653
00:34:42.070 --> 00:34:42.970
Oh, my gosh.

654
00:34:43.110 --> 00:34:45.170
There are so many delicious moments in this.

655
00:34:45.330 --> 00:34:47.410
I do not know how you guys got through this.

656
00:34:47.530 --> 00:34:49.610
Well, I'm going to say that a lot through this episode.

657
00:34:49.610 --> 00:34:54.150
Because there are scenes that were just the four of you that are some of the funniest

658
00:34:54.150 --> 00:34:55.070
scenes I've ever seen.

659
00:34:55.230 --> 00:34:57.090
And I don't know how you got through this one.

660
00:34:57.330 --> 00:35:03.310
This is the scene where for the longest time, there's just a camera on a tripod pointed at

661
00:35:03.310 --> 00:35:03.750
the bed.

662
00:35:04.050 --> 00:35:04.530
Yeah.

663
00:35:05.130 --> 00:35:07.090
Which Jim and Pam clock immediately.

664
00:35:07.090 --> 00:35:08.450
And so does the audience.

665
00:35:09.150 --> 00:35:12.490
And then Jan says, Michael, I told you to put that away.

666
00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:14.190
And then she swats him on the butt.

667
00:35:14.450 --> 00:35:15.490
It's so uncomfortable.

668
00:35:15.830 --> 00:35:18.590
She swats him on the butt.

669
00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:26.070
And then we learned that Michael sleeps on a bench at the foot of the bed.

670
00:35:26.290 --> 00:35:33.650
And Angela, I can't even tell you how hard we laughed every time Steve Carell curled up

671
00:35:33.650 --> 00:35:34.230
on that bench.

672
00:35:34.250 --> 00:35:35.370
When he was like, no, I fit.

673
00:35:35.490 --> 00:35:36.950
Look, it's actually pretty comfortable.

674
00:35:37.150 --> 00:35:39.410
He just curls up like a baby on it.

675
00:35:39.630 --> 00:35:43.350
Well, Paul shared with us the inspiration behind the bench.

676
00:35:43.350 --> 00:35:44.630
And it's so great.

677
00:35:44.850 --> 00:35:45.630
Here it is.

678
00:35:46.190 --> 00:35:53.190
So the whole thing with Michael and Jan and that weird bench that he sleeps on at the end

679
00:35:53.190 --> 00:36:00.310
of the bed came from when I was working as a script reader in the early eighties for a

680
00:36:00.310 --> 00:36:02.090
Hollywood, big Hollywood producer.

681
00:36:02.670 --> 00:36:05.050
And I befriended this woman who worked in his company.

682
00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:10.590
And at one point we were hanging out and we went by her house to pick something up.

683
00:36:10.970 --> 00:36:14.570
And I went in to there and they had this open bedroom plan.

684
00:36:14.750 --> 00:36:17.910
And I looked and there was this giant bed, like a king size bed.

685
00:36:18.010 --> 00:36:21.710
But then next to the bed was this little tiny cot.

686
00:36:22.870 --> 00:36:25.350
And I remember saying like, oh, what's that?

687
00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:26.930
And she got very serious.

688
00:36:27.070 --> 00:36:31.010
And she said like, look, when you get in a relationship, you have to make sure that you're

689
00:36:31.010 --> 00:36:35.850
able to be intimate with somebody and share things with them.

690
00:36:35.990 --> 00:36:43.450
And what it turned out was that when once they were done with their time together in bed,

691
00:36:43.450 --> 00:36:50.030
she had to roll off onto this cot and sleep by herself because this guy couldn't handle

692
00:36:50.030 --> 00:36:52.330
having anybody else in his bed with him.

693
00:36:53.110 --> 00:36:56.770
And I just thought that was the saddest thing I've ever seen in my life.

694
00:36:56.770 --> 00:37:01.650
And so I remember when we were scouting that location and there was that little stool at

695
00:37:01.650 --> 00:37:06.250
the end or that bench at the end of the bed, I can't remember if it was in the script or

696
00:37:06.250 --> 00:37:08.630
if we took it and ran with it.

697
00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:12.330
But I remember saying to Lee and Gene, like I told them that story.

698
00:37:12.570 --> 00:37:16.270
I said like, how funny, we have to have this thing where Michael can't sleep in the bed.

699
00:37:17.950 --> 00:37:19.290
And so that's what we did.

700
00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:24.110
And I just remember when we were shooting it, it made me laugh so much because, you know,

701
00:37:24.110 --> 00:37:26.710
the way that Steve Carell would curl up on that bench.

702
00:37:26.990 --> 00:37:30.790
And my whole thing was like, OK, we got to hang on this as long as we can.

703
00:37:30.990 --> 00:37:34.390
And so, you know, we have Jan going like, oh, he fits perfectly.

704
00:37:34.550 --> 00:37:34.930
Ha ha ha.

705
00:37:35.170 --> 00:37:41.050
And I just tried to get every last frame out of everyone just staring at Michael on this

706
00:37:41.050 --> 00:37:41.530
bench.

707
00:37:42.350 --> 00:37:45.730
And it's silent and it's just completely uncomfortable.

708
00:37:45.730 --> 00:37:47.810
And it makes me laugh still to this day.

709
00:37:49.290 --> 00:37:59.810
Now, lady, that clip suggests that maybe this was the condo guy's bed and bench, because

710
00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:05.410
he said when they scouted the location, they saw the bench or maybe just a bench and then

711
00:38:05.410 --> 00:38:06.570
we recreated it.

712
00:38:06.730 --> 00:38:09.390
But I have a bench at the end of my bed.

713
00:38:09.510 --> 00:38:10.590
No one sleeps on it.

714
00:38:10.690 --> 00:38:12.510
But this is a very popular thing.

715
00:38:12.670 --> 00:38:16.290
I have an upholstered bench at the foot of my bed as well.

716
00:38:16.290 --> 00:38:18.490
It's where I like sort of lay my robe at night.

717
00:38:18.650 --> 00:38:20.030
You know, I like kick off my slippers.

718
00:38:20.190 --> 00:38:21.310
I put my robe on the bench.

719
00:38:21.530 --> 00:38:21.710
Yeah.

720
00:38:21.870 --> 00:38:23.250
And it's all there for me in the morning.

721
00:38:23.890 --> 00:38:26.590
My bench could not fit a grown man.

722
00:38:27.170 --> 00:38:28.670
It's a tiny little bench.

723
00:38:28.990 --> 00:38:33.430
I actually really looked at that bench and I was like, oh, they found a wide bench.

724
00:38:33.670 --> 00:38:35.110
That's a substantial bench.

725
00:38:35.550 --> 00:38:36.450
Yes, exactly.

726
00:38:37.070 --> 00:38:43.750
It's just big enough that you believe it, but still small enough that it's just so sad.

727
00:38:43.750 --> 00:38:44.310
Yeah.

728
00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:49.150
And also, just again, Michael has no space in his own home.

729
00:38:49.430 --> 00:38:55.730
She has both guest rooms she's using, and now he doesn't even get to sleep in his bed.

730
00:38:55.750 --> 00:38:57.170
It's so sad.

731
00:38:57.350 --> 00:38:58.350
It's tragic.

732
00:38:58.690 --> 00:39:00.490
And now I've got a fun fact.

733
00:39:00.930 --> 00:39:09.810
